  "nameStory": "The Neighborly Mining Bee gets its friendly name from its social nesting habits - while each female digs her own individual burrow, they often cluster their underground homes together like a cozy neighborhood. The 'mining' part of the name comes from their impressive excavation skills, as females tunnel into the ground to create branching galleries for their eggs.",
  "description": "The Neighborly Mining Bee is a small, fuzzy native bee that spends most of its life underground in self-dug tunnels. These solitary bees are about the size of a small honeybee, with a compact, robust body covered in soft hairs that help them collect pollen. Females are excellent diggers, using their strong legs to excavate burrows in bare or sparsely vegetated soil. Like all mining bees, they're important early-season pollinators that emerge when many flowers first bloom in spring.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Small mound of excavated soil",
      "description": "Look for tiny volcano-shaped piles of fresh, fine soil with a pencil-width hole in the center - these mark active burrow entrances",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Compact, fuzzy body",
      "description": "Smaller than a honeybee with dense, soft hair that feels like velvet when gently touched; hair color varies from golden-brown to grayish",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Fast, low flight pattern",
      "description": "Flies quickly in zigzag patterns close to the ground, often hovering over potential nesting sites with an audible buzzing",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Pollen-heavy legs",
      "description": "Females carry large, colorful pollen loads on their hind legs that make them look like they're wearing fuzzy pants",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Early spring activity",
      "description": "Active when temperatures reach about 60°F, often seen on cool mornings when other bees are still inactive",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Neighborly Mining Bees prefer well-drained, sandy or loamy soils in open areas like gardens, yards, parks, and field edges throughout New England. They especially favor south-facing slopes and areas with sparse vegetation where digging is easier. You'll often find their burrows clustered in the same patch of bare soil, creating small \"bee neighborhoods\" that return to the same spots year after year.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Peak activity from April through May when adults emerge and begin nesting. Look for fresh soil mounds and bees actively digging or foraging on early flowers like dandelions and fruit tree blossoms.",
    "summer": "Adult activity winds down by early June as the generation completes its life cycle. Burrows remain visible but inactive, with new larvae developing underground.",
    "fall": "Burrows appear abandoned with weathered soil mounds. New adults are developing underground but won't emerge until next spring.",
    "winter": "Completely inactive with next year's generation overwintering as mature larvae in sealed underground cells, waiting for spring warmth."
  },
  "funFact": "A single female Neighborly Mining Bee can move up to 40 times her own body weight in soil while excavating her burrow - imagine if humans could effortlessly dig tunnels while carrying around 6,000 pounds of dirt!",
  "confusionNotes": "Often mistaken for small honeybees or bumblebees, but mining bees create distinctive soil mounds at burrow entrances that honeybees never make. Remember: if you see tiny dirt volcanoes with bees nearby, you're looking at miners, not honey-makers.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Despite being called 'mining' bees, they're actually beneficial garden visitors that improve soil aeration through their digging and provide excellent pollination services. Many people mistakenly think they're harmful because of the soil mounds, but they're actually helping both plants and soil health.",
  "compareHints": "Neighborly Mining Bees are distinguished from other Andrena species by their relatively small size and preference for clustered nesting sites. Unlike the larger Hairy-banded Mining Bee which has distinct white hair bands, or specialist species like the Aster Mining Bee which focuses on specific flowers, Neighborly Mining Bees are generalist foragers that visit many different spring blooms. Their burrow entrances are typically smaller and more numerous in a given area compared to their larger mining bee relatives.",
